'''Just refine it a little.

If we keep one fully sorted array/list, then when a new element comes:

we can find its correct position fast
but to actually place it there, elements after that position may need to shift

So worst case insertion becomes O(n).

That is the problem.

But with two heaps, we are not trying to maintain full sorted order.

We are only maintaining enough structure to answer the median:

left side gives the largest value from the lower part
right side gives the smallest value from the upper part

Those are exactly the 1 or 2 center-side values we need.

So yes, the idea is:

full sorted order is more than what median needs
maintaining more information costs more
two heaps maintain only the useful middle boundary
that is why insertion becomes cheaper, O(log n)'''



'''No, it is not overfitting.

It is actually a very general pattern.

What is true is this:

the exact two-heaps median template is specialized for median-like problems
but the thinking behind it is very general
Why it is not overfitting

This solution is based on a broad idea:

Do not maintain more information than the query needs.
Maintain only the boundary information that matters.

That is a very reusable idea in data structures.

In this problem:

full sorted order is too much
median only needs the middle boundary
so we maintain just enough structure to recover that boundary

That is not overfitting.
That is good data structure design.

What is the general pattern here?

The pattern is:

identify what the query really needs
avoid storing stronger structure than necessary
maintain only the useful summary or boundary
choose data structures that make updates cheap and queries cheap

That pattern is used everywhere.

Where this same thinking appears
1. Running median

This exact problem

Maintain:

lower half
upper half

Because median depends on the split.

2. Kth largest in a stream

You do not keep everything fully sorted.

You keep only a min heap of size k.

Why?

Because the query only needs the kth largest, not full order.

Same idea:

maintain only what matters
3. Top K frequent elements

You do not sort all frequencies fully every time.

You use:

heap
bucket sort
frequency map

Again:

only preserve enough structure for top-k query
4. Merge intervals / meeting rooms style problems

You often do not keep every detail perfectly arranged.
You keep the key boundary:

earliest ending time
current overlap count
active intervals

Again:

maintain the critical frontier, not everything
5. Sliding window maximum

You use a deque.

Why not sort each window?

Because the query only needs the max of the current active window.

So again:

maintain only useful candidates
throw away useless order information

Very similar philosophy.

6. Dijkstra / Prim

Priority queues again.

Why?

Because at each step, you only need the next best frontier node or edge.

Not full sorted structure of everything.

Again:

maintain query-relevant boundary
What is specific and what is general?
Specific

“Use one max heap and one min heap for median in a stream.”

That exact template is specific.

General

“Split the data around the query boundary and maintain only the important edge values.”

That idea is highly general.

A better way to think about it

This is not overfitting.

This is a case of:

the query shape determines the data structure shape

Examples:

median query → split around center
kth largest query → keep best k
max-in-window query → keep only useful max candidates
minimum active end time query → min heap of ends

This is exactly how strong problem solving works.

Why it feels problem-specific

It feels specific because median is a very special statistic.

Median is about the center split.

So the solution mirrors that very directly:

left side
right side
center boundary

That direct match can feel “too custom.”

But that is actually a good sign, not a bad sign.

The best data structure solutions often mirror the math definition of the query.

When would it actually be overfitting?

It would be overfitting if the method depended on accidental details of the problem statement that do not matter.

For example:

relying on some arbitrary test pattern
using hacks that only work because of one narrow constraint
building a structure that cannot justify itself from the query logic

That is not what is happening here.

